Core state variables

Let’s define a minimal but powerful field set over space (\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3) and time (t):

Material density field[ \rho(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

Stress tensor field[ \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

Damage / void field (0 = intact, 1 = fully failed)[ D(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

Signal potential field (piezo/gradient signal)[ \phi(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

Hardening agent concentration (microfluidic / reactive material)[ c(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

1. Analyze_Surrounding_Gradients

This becomes a local operator that reads spatial derivatives of the fields:

[ \text{Analyze_Surrounding_Gradients}(\mathbf{x}, t) \rightarrow {\nabla \rho, \nabla D, \nabla \phi, \nabla c, \nabla p, \nabla T} ]

where (p(\mathbf{x}, t)) is pressure and (T(\mathbf{x}, t)) temperature if needed.

Concretely:

Stress gradient magnitude[ g_\sigma(\mathbf{x}, t) = |\nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x}, t)| ]

Damage gradient[ g_D(\mathbf{x}, t) = |\nabla D(\mathbf{x}, t)| ]

Signal gradient[ g_\phi(\mathbf{x}, t) = |\nabla \phi(\mathbf{x}, t)| ]

These scalars feed the branching/repair rules.

2. Signal generation (piezoelectric-like)

Local stress generates signal potential:

[ \phi_t(\mathbf{x}, t) = \alpha_\sigma , |\boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x}, t)| - \beta_\phi , \phi(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

(\alpha_\sigma): coupling from stress to signal

(\beta_\phi): decay / dissipation

3. Microfluidic / agent transport

Hardening agent moves along signal and pressure gradients:

[ \frac{\partial c}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot \left( D_c \nabla c + \kappa_\phi , c \nabla \phi + \kappa_p , c \nabla p \right) - R(\mathbf{x}, t) ]

(D_c): diffusion coefficient

(\kappa_\phi): chemotactic/gradient-following strength toward (\nabla \phi)

(\kappa_p): flow toward pressure gradients

(R): reaction term (consumption when material hardens)

4. Growth / hardening update

Material density increases where stress is high and hardening agent is available:

[ \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} = \gamma_g , f_\text{grow}\big(|\boldsymbol{\sigma}|, c\big) - \gamma_e , f_\text{erode}(D) ]

Example choice:

[ f_\text{grow} = \max\left(0, |\boldsymbol{\sigma}| - \sigma_\text{th}\right) \cdot \frac{c}{c + c_0} ]

(\sigma_\text{th}): stress threshold for growth

(c_0): saturation constant

(\gamma_g): growth rate

(\gamma_e): erosion/decay rate

Damage evolves from overstress:

[ \frac{\partial D}{\partial t} = \eta_D , \max\left(0, |\boldsymbol{\sigma}| - \sigma_\text{fail}\right) - \eta_H , h(\rho, c) ]

(\sigma_\text{fail}): failure threshold

(\eta_D): damage accumulation rate

(\eta_H): healing rate

(h(\rho, c)): healing effectiveness (e.g., increases with (\rho) and (c))

5. Mechanical equilibrium (stress field)

You can plug in standard linear elasticity:

[ \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x}, t) + \mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}, t) = \mathbf{0} ]

with constitutive law:

[ \boldsymbol{\sigma} = \mathbb{C}(\rho) : \boldsymbol{\varepsilon} ]

(\mathbb{C}(\rho)): stiffness tensor depending on density (denser → stiffer)

(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}): strain tensor from displacement field (\mathbf{u})

6. Discrete update scheme (simulation-ready)

In a numerical grid or mesh:

Given (\rho^n, D^n, \phi^n, c^n) at time step (n)

Solve elasticity for (\boldsymbol{\sigma}^n)

Update (\phi^{n+1}) using the signal equation

Update (c^{n+1}) using the transport equation

Update (\rho^{n+1}, D^{n+1}) using growth/damage equations

Repeat

This loop is your Growth Logic Engine in mathematical form.

